/h1><p>To explore how these patterns are visualised using the pathway and score sequencing data, and how a layer of abstraction not normally associated with Deborah Hay's practice can reveal unseen regularities in the work.</p>"},"id":"535503636d30347d741f0000","poster_image":{"url":"/assets/fallback/default-cdee06016c3ca15e5810fcda0a4c5e44.png","small":{"url":"/assets/fallback/small_default-b553e49f4bf6795c93d62ecc6a576ac4.png"},"medium":{"url":"/assets/fallback/medium_default-cdee06016c3ca15e5810fcda0a4c5e44.png"}},"x":28,"y":4,"width":2,"height":2},{"title":"Cell describing pattern discoveries","description":"","type":"html","additional_fields":{"html-content":"<h1>Deborah Hay's choreographic approach</h1><p>...embraces variability and the \"disruption of linearity\" (see the <a href=\"#/set/introduction-to-concepts\">Introduction to Concepts</a>)</br></br>The notes in 'No Time To Fly' are explicit. On Tab 17 of the score text: \"The movement may change, but the choreography itself does not change.\" By filming as many versions as possible, and extracting two kinds of data from this material (the pathway and score sequence for each performance) patterns began to show up.</p>"},"id":"5355065b6d30347d6e120000","poster_image":{"url":"/assets/fallback/default-cdee06016c3ca15e5810fcda0a4c5e44.png","small":{"url":"/assets/fallback/small_default-b553e49f4bf6795c93d62ecc6a576ac4.png"},"medium":{"url":"/assets/fallback/medium_default-cdee06016c3ca15e5810fcda0a4c5e44.png"}},"x":28,"y":0,"width":2,"height":3},{"title":"Jeanine Durning","description":"Recording No. 3, Following Camera, 22 April 2011, Frankfurt (Day 5, Take 3)","type":"recording","additional_fields":{"xxx-recording":"D05T03","xxx-videoId":"258","xxx-content-url":"modules/flowplayer/index.html","xxx-file-name":"D05T03_Janine_sync_AJA_1","iframe-src":"/dh/dhay/player/?rec=D05T03&vid=69397&file=D05T03_Janine_sync_AJA_1&gid=24&perf=jeaninedurning","autoload":"0","solo":"24","attr-allowfullscreen":"allowfullscreen